<!--
  Phase 5 - custom Wazuh detections.

  STATUS: UNVERIFIED. Not one of these rules has fired yet. By this repo's own
  standard a rule without a passing test is a guess, so none of them may be
  called a detection until `scripts/verify/05-detections.sh` shows it firing,
  read back from the indexer.

  Why these exist: ET Open produced zero alerts on a 100-port nmap of DC01 and
  contributed two false positives plus eight INFO-class hits in 63.6 hours
  (detection/README.md, 4.3). It is a perimeter ruleset aimed at an internal
  flat network. These rules are the detection capability on this lab, not a
  bonus layer on top of it.

  Deploy to:  /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml
  Then:       sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager
  This file is canonical; the deployed copy must match it, the same contract
  03-ids.sh enforces for the Suricata local rules.

  IDs 100100-100103 sit in Wazuh's 100000-120000 user range.

  Field paths assume the windows_eventchannel decoder. Verify each against a
  real event with `wazuh-logtest -v` before trusting a green result - a rule
  that never matches and a rule that never had a matching event look identical
  from the outside.
-->

<group name="homelab,attack,">

  <!--
    100100 - Kerberoasting (T1558.003)

    4769 is a service ticket request: one of the noisiest events in AD. The
    signal is the encryption type. Server 2019/2022 negotiate AES by default,
    so an RC4 (0x17) request for a user-account SPN is anomalous and is what
    Rubeus/GetUserSPNs ask for to get a crackable ticket.

    Exclusions, both in the serviceName pcre2:
      - krbtgt        : the TGT itself, always present
      - names ending $: computer accounts, which legitimately use RC4 in places
  -->
  <!--
    if_sid 60106, measured 2026-09-18, not guessed.

    The first version of this rule was atomic (decoded_as windows_eventchannel
    only). It never fired. The manager API confirmed it was loaded and enabled
    with every field condition parsed correctly, and a 4769 with
    ticketEncryptionType 0x17 and serviceName svc_test was in the indexer -
    a match on all three conditions. Stock rule 60106 alerted instead, at
    level 3. local_rules.xml loads last and analysisd stops at the first
    matching atomic rule, so this rule was never reached. Chaining to the
    stock parent makes it a child, which is evaluated and overrides.

    60106 is the measured owner of 4769 on this manager: 95 of 95 docs.
  -->
  <rule id="100100" level="12">
    <if_sid>60106</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4769$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.ticketEncryptionType">^0x17$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.serviceName" type="pcre2">^(?!krbtgt$)(?!.*\$$).+</field>
    <description>Kerberoasting: RC4 service ticket requested for SPN $(win.eventdata.serviceName) by $(win.eventdata.targetUserName)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1558.003</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    100101 - AS-REP roasting (T1558.004)

    4768 with preAuthType 0 means a TGT was issued with Kerberos pre-auth
    disabled - the account is roastable without any credential. Normal accounts
    report preAuthType 2 (encrypted timestamp). A 0 is either a genuinely
    misconfigured account or an attacker enumerating for one.

    Note: this fires on the account being *usable* this way, so it will also
    catch the test user created to prove it. That is correct, not a false
    positive - remove the flag after testing.

    BLOCKED ON TELEMETRY, measured 2026-09-18. There are zero 4768 documents
    in the entire retention. Not "none from the trigger" - none ever. 4769 is
    collected (95 docs), but 4769 is Kerberos Service Ticket Operations and
    4768 is Kerberos Authentication Service: a different audit subcategory,
    and it is not enabled on DC01. This is why the runas trigger produced a
    4625 instead of a 4768.

    Consequence: this rule has no measured stock parent, because no 4768 has
    ever reached the manager to show which rule owns it. The other three rules
    were chained to parents measured from real docs. Guessing a parent here
    would be inventing the one fact the chain depends on. So this rule stays
    atomic and stays UNVERIFIED until 4768 is actually being collected - then
    measure the owner and chain it the same way.

    Update 2026-09-18: 4768 IS collected. auditpol on DC01 reports Kerberos
    Authentication Service = Success and Failure, and a real AS-REQ sent from
    the host (kinit nosuchuser@HOMELAB.LAN against KDC 192.168.50.10:88) landed
    in wazuh-alerts-* 14 seconds later under stock rule 60104, status 0x6. The
    earlier "0 docs all-time" reading was meaningless: the manager runs
    logall/logall_json = no at log_alert_level 3, so wazuh-alerts-* holds alerts
    and not events, and a *successful* 4768 alerts on nothing and is discarded.

    60104 is the owner of the FAILURE case only. A pre-auth-disabled AS-REQ
    succeeds, and a success 4768 has not been observed here - it produces no
    alert under any stock rule, which is why this rule stayed atomic and why a
    kinit as svc_test with pre-auth off left no doc behind. Do not chain this to
    60104: that is the failure parent, and using it would be the guess this
    repo keeps paying for.

    To settle it, the manager needs somewhere to record non-alerting events:
      /var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf -> <global><logall_json>yes</logall_json>
      systemctl restart wazuh-manager
    then re-run the AS-REP trigger and read /var/ossec/logs/archives/ for the
    4768, measure its owner (possibly none), and chain accordingly. Costs disk;
    see the RAM budget in ROADMAP before enabling it permanently.
  -->
  <rule id="100101" level="12">
    <decoded_as>windows_eventchannel</decoded_as>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4768$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.preAuthType">^0$</field>
    <description>AS-REP roasting: TGT issued without Kerberos pre-authentication for $(win.eventdata.targetUserName)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1558.004</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    100102 - Encoded PowerShell (T1059.001)

    Rewritten 2026-09-18 against a measured event. The previous version matched
    -enc / -EncodedCommand / FromBase64String against 4104's scriptBlockText,
    on the reasoning that 4104 "survives encoding". It does - and that is
    exactly why it could never fire. 4104 logs the *decoded* block: running
    powershell.exe -enc on Get-Date writes the text "Get-Date". The encoding
    marker lives on the command line, which 4104 does not carry. The rule was
    searching decoded output for the artefact decoding removes. Script-block
    logging was on the whole time (EnableScriptBlockLogging = 1); that was
    never the blocker.

    4688 carries commandLine. First attempt chained this to 92027 and did not
    fire. Cause, measured 2026-09-18 15:31: 92027 owns **Sysmon Event ID 1** on
    channel Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational, not 4688. The parent was read
    off a rule.id aggregation over a commandLine query without checking the
    eventID of those docs - so the rule required ^4688$ under a parent that
    only ever carries EID 1, and could not match anything by construction.

    A single `powershell.exe -enc` run produces two separate documents:
      4688 on Security                    -> stock 67027 (level 3)
      EID 1 on Sysmon/Operational         -> stock 92027 (level 4)
    67027 is the measured owner of the 4688 and is the correct parent. It fires
    on every process creation (2691 docs); that is normal for an if_sid anchor -
    the field match below is what narrows it, not the parent.

    Known limit unchanged: a string match on the launcher shape. Case and
    whitespace are handled; splitting the literal is not. It raises the cost of
    casual obfuscation, it does not stop a deliberate operator. 4688 also only
    sees the invocation, not the payload - pair with 4104 when reading an alert.
  -->
  <rule id="100102" level="10">
    <if_sid>67027</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4688$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(-enc\s|-e\s+[A-Za-z0-9+/=]{16,}|-encodedcommand|frombase64string)</field>
    <description>Encoded PowerShell executed on $(win.system.computer)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    100103 - Service installed with an interpreter payload (T1543.003)

    7045 is Service Control Manager recording a new service. Service creation is
    a normal admin act, so plain 7045 is not a detection. The signal is an
    imagePath that runs an interpreter rather than a binary - the shape PsExec,
    Metasploit service payloads and most persistence tooling leave behind.

    PREREQUISITE, and a real one: 7045 is on the *System* channel, not Security.
    02-telemetry.sh only proves Security and Sysmon/Operational are collected.
    If System is not in the shared agent.conf this rule cannot fire and will
    look exactly like "no attacks happened". 05-detections.sh checks for the
    channel first for that reason.
  -->
  <!--
    if_sid 61138: measured owner of 7045 on this manager, 2 of 2 docs -
    including the KsID boot driver on 09-18 and the phase5test service
    created by the trigger at 13:38:59.
  -->
  <rule id="100103" level="12">
    <if_sid>61138</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^7045$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.imagePath" type="pcre2">(?i)(powershell|cmd\.exe|rundll32|regsvr32|mshta|wscript|cscript|%COMSPEC%|-enc)</field>
    <description>Service $(win.eventdata.serviceName) installed with interpreter image path: $(win.eventdata.imagePath)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1543.003</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    100104 - RESERVED: internal port scan from pfSense filterlog (T1046)

    Not written yet, deliberately. This is the detection that closes the hole
    Phase 4 measured - ET Open saw nothing on a 100-port nmap of the DC - so it
    matters more than the four above. It will be a frequency rule over rule
    87701 (pfSense firewall drop), grouped by srcip.

    Blocked on: confirming block events reach the indexer at all. Decoding is
    proven working via `pf` decoder at level 0 for pass traffic; 87701 is the
    only pfSense rule above level 0, and it has not yet been observed firing.
    Writing this rule before that measurement would repeat the mistake this
    session already made twice.

    ==== CANDIDATE, NOT DEPLOYED ===========================================
    Drafted offline so the next lab-up session is edit and test, not author
    from scratch. Do not uncomment until ALL THREE are true:

      1. pfSense has a hostname on the wire (issue 13) and a block event has
         been captured with `tcpdump -lni virbr-lab -A udp port 514`.
      2. That event is confirmed to pre-decode: `wazuh-logtest` run against
         the line as the INDEXER stored it - not a hand-typed line (issue 7).
      3. 87701 is observed firing in wazuh-alerts-* at least once. If the
         parent id differs on 4.14.3, fix <if_matched_sid> before testing.

    Every number below is a guess until 05-detections.sh shows it green:
    frequency/timeframe are placeholders sized for a 100-port nmap (which is
    what Phase 4 ran and ET Open missed), not measured against this network's
    ambient block rate. Expect to tune both, and expect the LAN allow rule's
    logging volume (see the Volume warning in docs/issues.md) to matter here.

    <rule id="100104" level="10" frequency="15" timeframe="60">
      <if_matched_sid>87701</if_matched_sid>
      <same_source_ip />
      <description>Internal port scan: $(srcip) hit 15+ blocked ports/services in 60s</description>
      <mitre>
        <id>T1046</id>
      </mitre>
    </rule>

    Test, once deployed (from the host, no attacker VM):
      nmap -Pn -p 1-100 192.168.50.10
    then: ./scripts/verify/05-detections.sh
    ========================================================================
  -->

</group>
